=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router6
diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** The iptables FORWARD chain on router6, specifically four rules keyed on inbound interface `eth2` (the `r6_r3` link connecting router6 to router3).

**Mechanism:** router6 has a blanket ACL that drops all forwarded traffic arriving on its `eth2` interface: separate DROP rules for TCP and UDP, followed by a catch-all DROP for all protocols. A single narrow ACCEPT exception (TCP port 80, source 172.16.10.0/24, destination 172.18.51.1) precedes the drops but is effectively irrelevant for the affected traffic. Because `eth2` is the only WAN-facing link between router6 and the rest of the routing domain (via router3), any return traffic from 192.168.200.x destined for 172.18.51.x must traverse router3→router6 eth2 — and is silently discarded there. The 18-packet/1512-byte drop counter on rule 4 confirms live ICMP echo replies are being killed, matching exactly the ping timeout observed from pc15 to pc12 (192.168.200.12). Outbound traffic from pc15 exits router6 normally via eth2 toward router3; only the return path is blocked, producing a one-way black hole.

**Localization:** router6, FORWARD chain, rules 2–4 (`-A FORWARD -i eth2 -p tcp -j DROP`, `-p udp -j DROP`, and the catch-all `-j DROP`). Interface eth2 maps to collision domain `r6_r3`.

**Root cause:** Misconfigured iptables on router6 blocks all forwarded traffic ingressing on eth2, cutting off return-path connectivity for the entire 172.18.51.0/24 subnet from any network reachable via router3 (including 192.168.200.0/24 at Location C).

**Fix:** Remove the three DROP rules (rules 2, 3, 4) from the FORWARD chain on router6 with `iptables -D FORWARD -i eth2 -p tcp -j DROP`, `iptables -D FORWARD -i eth2 -p udp -j DROP`, and `iptables -D FORWARD -i eth2 -j DROP`.
=== END ===